course: everyday
lesson: 208
title: Teeth, nails, baths and toilets
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Good habits look after the body: brushing well, cleaning between teeth with floss, short clean nails and a clean toilet for the next person.
explain: After brushing you spit out the toothpaste but do not rinse with lots of water straight away, because the fluoride left on the teeth keeps protecting them for longer.
explain: A small child can drown in a few centimetres of water in seconds, so a little brother or sister is never left alone in the bath: you call an adult.
explain: A boy in Texas has a numbing injection at the dentist; his lip feels fat and strange for a few hours, so he takes care not to bite or chew it, because he could hurt it without feeling any pain.
explain: Fingernails are cut following their curve, toenails straight across, so the corners do not grow into the skin.
fact: After brushing, spit out the toothpaste but do not rinse your mouth with water straight away.
fact: Fluoride left on the teeth keeps protecting them for longer.
fact: Brush your tongue gently too, because germs on the tongue cause bad breath.
fact: Food stuck between teeth is removed with floss, never with a fork, pin or other sharp object, which can cut gums and chip teeth.
fact: After a numbing injection, the lip and cheek feel fat and strange for a few hours, and you must not bite or chew them.
fact: A small child must never be left alone in a bath, because small children can drown in a few centimetres of water in seconds.
fact: Short showers of about five minutes save water and energy.
fact: Nails are kept short and clean, with dirt scrubbed out from under them, because germs collect there.
fact: Fingernails are cut following their curve and toenails straight across, with nail clippers or by an adult.
fact: Toenails cut too short or curved can grow into the skin.
fact: Biting nails puts germs from under them into the mouth, and torn, bleeding skin round a nail can get infected, so tell a grown-up if it bleeds.
fact: Safety glasses are worn when you are told to during experiments, because chemicals can hurt eyes for life.
fact: If you miss the toilet or leave it messy, you clean it up or tell an adult, so the next person finds it clean.
exercise: You have finished brushing your teeth. What do you do with the toothpaste? | Spit it out but do not rinse with lots of water straight away | Rinse with three full cups of water | Swallow it all | Keep brushing until it is gone
exercise: Why should you not rinse straight after brushing? | The fluoride left on the teeth keeps protecting them | Water makes your teeth softer | Rinsing makes your gums bleed | Toothpaste tastes better left in
exercise: Why should you brush your tongue gently too? | Germs on the tongue cause bad breath | It makes your tongue grow a little longer | It helps you taste sweet things better | The tongue holds most of your fluoride
exercise: A piece of meat is stuck between your teeth. What do you use? | Dental floss | A fork from the kitchen | A small safety pin | A pencil point
exercise: Why should you never pick your teeth with a pin? | Sharp things can cut the gums and chip teeth | Pins are far too slow to get the bits of food out | It makes the food go further in | Teeth are too hard for pins to touch
exercise: The dentist gave you a numbing injection. What should you be careful about? | Not biting or chewing your numb lip and cheek | Not talking to anybody for a whole day afterwards | Not drinking any water for a week | Not brushing your teeth for a month
exercise: Why can a numb lip get hurt without you noticing? | You cannot feel pain there for a few hours | The injection makes the lip weak and floppy forever | Numb lips heal by themselves at once | Your teeth get sharper after an injection
exercise: Mum is cooking and your two-year-old brother is in the bath. She asks you to fetch a towel from upstairs. What do you do? | Stay with him and call your mum, so he is never alone in the bath | Go, since the water is not deep | Turn the tap on so he has fun while you go | Leave the door open and go quickly
exercise: Why must a small child never be alone in a bath? | Small children can drown in a few centimetres of water in seconds | The water might get cold | They may splash the floor | They could use too much soap
exercise: About how long is a water-saving shower? | Around five minutes | Around half an hour or so | Around one minute only | Around an hour or more
exercise: Your fingernails are long and have dirt under them. What do you do? | Cut them short and scrub under them | Leave them alone until they break off by themselves | Paint over the dirt | Bite them off
exercise: How are toenails cut? | Straight across, with clippers or by an adult | Very short and rounded off deeply at the corners | With kitchen scissors as short as you can | Torn off with your fingers
exercise: Why are toenails not cut too short or curved? | They can grow into the skin | They will stop growing for good | They turn a different colour | They get too hard to cut again
exercise: Your science teacher says to put on safety glasses for an experiment. Why? | Chemicals can hurt eyes for life | Glasses help you read the instructions | They make the experiment work faster | It is only to look like a scientist
exercise: You missed the toilet a little. What do you do? | Clean it up or tell an adult | Leave it there for the next person | Shut the lid quietly and walk away | Flush and pretend it was not you
test: A girl in Leeds brushes, spits, then gulps lots of water to rinse. What advice would her dentist give? | Spit, but skip the big rinse so the fluoride stays on | Rinse three times with warm water to be extra clean | Never spit, just swallow | Brush with water and no toothpaste
test: A boy's friends say his breath smells even though he brushes his teeth. What might he be forgetting? | To brush his tongue gently | To brush his hair every morning | To wash his hands | To drink a glass of milk at bedtime
test: Which tool is safe for cleaning between your teeth? | Floss | A needle | A toothpick-sharp knife | A paper clip
test: After the dentist, a girl in Ottawa cannot feel her lip. What should she do while eating? | Eat carefully so she does not bite her numb lip | Chew really hard to wake the lip up again | Eat only crunchy food | Bite her lip now and then to test if it still feels nothing
test: A baby sister is splashing in a shallow bath when the doorbell rings. What is true? | She must not be left alone even for a moment | Shallow water is safe to leave her in for a minute | Babies float, so it is fine | Only deep baths are dangerous for babies
test: Which helps save water and energy at home? | Short showers of about five minutes | Long showers to get yourself really clean | Leaving the tap running while you brush | Filling the bath to the top every day
test: Which is true about cutting nails? | Fingernails follow the curve, toenails go straight across | Both are cut as short and as round as you can possibly get them | Toenails are cut in a deep curve | Nails never need cutting
test: A boy at a science club does not want to wear safety glasses because they look silly. Why should he? | A splash of chemicals could damage his eyes for life | The teacher just likes everyone to look the same in class | Safety glasses help him see colours | They are only needed for adults
test: At a friend's house you leave the toilet messy. Why should you clean it? | The next person should find a clean toilet | Toilets clean themselves anyway each time you flush | Only adults use that toilet | It is someone else's job, like the cleaner's
test: done brushing, rinse w water now? | Just spit, don't rinse straight away | Yes, rinse three times to get it all out | Yes, with hot water | Swallow the toothpaste instead
test: bit of chicken stuck in my teeth, gonna use a fork | Use floss, never a fork or pin | Yes, the fork's fine if you're careful | Use a pin, it's thinner | Leave it there for a week
test: dentist numbed my mouth, lip feels huge lol, can i have crisps | Wait, you might bite your lip or cheek | Yes, chew on the numb side, it's easier | Yes, you won't feel it so it's fine | Bite your lip to test it
test: lil sis in the bath, just popping out 4 a sec | Don't leave her alone, she can drown in seconds | Fine if it's only a minute | Fine if the water is shallow | Fine if she's singing
explain: A girl in New Zealand hardly brushed her teeth and drank fizzy drinks every day; at nine she needed several fillings and one tooth taken out.
explain: A boy in Canada did not wash his hands after the toilet before snack time; a stomach bug went round his class and many children missed days of school.
fact: Tooth decay is one of the most common illnesses in children, and bad decay can cause toothache, infection and lost teeth.
fact: Washing hands with soap after the toilet stops many stomach bugs that make whole classes ill.
test: too tired to brush teeth tonight, it's fine once yeah | Brush anyway, sugar on teeth all night causes decay | Yes, skipping is fine | Only brush in the morning | Rinse with juice instead
test: Why did half the class get sick after a boy skipped washing his hands? | Germs from his hands spread on shared things | Classes always get sick all at once in winter | Soap spreads germs | It was just the cold, rainy weather that week
test: my brother bites his nails till they bleed | Tell a grown-up, the bleeding bits can get infected | It's fine, nails grow back | Put tape on his mouth | Bite yours too
test: did u hear a toddler drowned in the bath when his mum went to get a towel for like a second?? what's the real lesson? | Never leave a small child alone in a bath, even briefly | Babies can only drown in deep baths, so keep the water low | Fill the bath higher so they float | It only happens if the child cannot sit up yet
test: my cousin said she picked her teeth with a pin and it went right through her gum and she had to have surgery. probably blown up? | The surgery part; but sharp things really cut gums, so use floss | That pins are sharp | That food gets stuck in teeth | That floss removes food from teeth
test: apparently that kid in our class has really bad breath cos he never brushes, everyone's saying it. pass it on? | No, that's hurtful gossip about him | Yes, so he finally brushes his teeth | Yes, it's just the truth | Yes, if you say it as a joke
exam: Identify the correct practice immediately following the brushing of the teeth. | The toothpaste is spat out without rinsing with water | The mouth is rinsed thoroughly with cold water at once | Toothpaste is swallowed for further protection | The mouth is rinsed with fruit juice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cutting of toenails? | They are cut straight across, lest they grow into the skin | They are cut in a deep curve, as close to the skin as can be managed | They are cut as short as is possible | They are torn by hand to avoid tools
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the supervision of small children in the bath. | A small child may drown in a few centimetres of water within seconds | A small child cannot drown in shallow water | Supervision is required only in swimming pools | A small child may be left alone briefly
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which the hands are washed with soap after use of the toilet. | It prevents the spread of germs causing stomach illnesses | It is a custom of long standing without any benefit to health | It is required only in schools | It removes the need to wash before meals
